      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this method might be problematic because I\u0027m not sure what it should do when the point is exactly on the border, and not sure how to name it in a way that would make that clear. In my own mind, though this might not be intuitive for everyone, I would see it as:\n\n  contains(pt) returns true if the point is on the border\n  intersects(pt) returns false if the point is on the border\n  \nSkRect::contains(x,y) tries to always be able to give the wrong answer, and returns true if the point on the top or left border, and false if it\u0027s on the right or bottom...\n\nWhat would you think of forcing the caller to create their own empty rect and then call contains() or intersects()? I think that would give the most readable code. And then, depending on your use case, you could call either:\n\n  contains(Rect::Point(pt))\n  intersects(Rect::Point(pt))\n  \n... and decide for yourself which edge behavior you want.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "f7552afdd47dd81e65636bdb9dbb10af50c7e422",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
